\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Composition and field multiplication}
\date{2026-10-20}


\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring. For
\vocabk{#{R}-modules}{ca-000X} #{M,N,P}, use
\vocabk{#{\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,N)}}{ca-001G} for additive,
scalar-preserving maps. Thus \vocabk{linearity}{ca-001F} fixes
#{\sigma=\operatorname{id}_R}. Given
#{A\in\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,N)} and
#{B\in\operatorname{Hom}_R(N,P)}, write
##{BA=B\circ A:M\to P.}
Composition is associative and #{R}-bilinear where the types match.
In particular, the \vocabk{endomorphisms}{ca-0015}
#{\operatorname{End}_R(M)=\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,M)} form an
associative unital \vocabk{#{R}-algebra}{ca-0016}, with unit #{I_M}
and central scalar map #{r\mapsto rI_M}.}

\p{On the \vocabk{vertex-field module}{fdip-0010}, each
#{f\in\mathcal F_R(V)} defines a \newvocab{multiplication operator}
##{M_f:\mathcal F_R(V)\to\mathcal F_R(V),\qquad M_f(g)=fg.}
The assignment #{f\mapsto M_f} is an injective #{R}-algebra map:
##{M_{f+g}=M_f+M_g,\quad M_{rf}=rM_f,\quad
   M_{fg}=M_fM_g,\quad M_1=I.}
The full endomorphism algebra need not be commutative; the
[shift relation](fdip-0013) and
[four-cycle calculation](fdip-0003) exhibit the obstruction.}

\proof{
\p{Pointwise addition and scalar multiplication make
#{\operatorname{Hom}_R(M,N)} a module: the linearity equations are
preserved by both operations. If #{A,B} are composable, then
#{BA(rx+y)=B(rAx+Ay)=rBAx+BAy}, so #{BA} is linear.
For a third composable map #{C},
#{(CB)A(x)=C(B(Ax))=C(BA)(x)}. The identity map is a two-sided unit.
For composable maps with common source and target,
##{\begin{aligned}
B(A+A')(x)&=B(Ax)+B(A'x),\\
(B+B')A(x)&=B(Ax)+B'(Ax),\\
B(rA)(x)&=rB(Ax),\qquad (rB)A(x)=rB(Ax).
\end{aligned}}
These prove distributivity and bilinearity. For an endomorphism #{A},
#{(rI)A(x)=rAx=A(rx)=A(rI)(x)}, proving centrality of the scalar map;
its unit, addition and product laws follow immediately by evaluation.}
\p{Pointwise multiplication makes #{M_f} linear. Evaluating the four
claimed identities on a field proves them using the ring laws. If
#{M_f=M_g}, applying both maps to the constant field #{1} yields
#{f=g}; this also holds when #{V} is empty or #{R} is the zero ring.}
}

\p{The Hom-module and algebra conventions are
\citet{Section 10.10, Tag 0581; Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks};
the field multiplication assertions are the direct calculation above.
Mathlib's \leanref{Module.End.mul_eq_comp} and
\leanref{Module.End.mul_apply} give this right-factor-first product.
These declarations do not assert that arbitrary maps with different
endpoints form one algebra, or that a formal generator presentation is
faithful.}
